course: dictionary
lesson: 5050
title: Formal words: inordinate
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grown-up because they belong to formal writing, such as reports, letters and exam answers. Inordinate is one of them. Context clues still help. «The school report mentioned an inordinate amount of homework.» The writer is complaining about too much homework, so inordinate must mean far too much.
explain: Ask: what would fit in this place? Try swapping in "far too much" and see if the sentence still makes sense. «Inordinate delays» means delays that are much longer than they should be.
explain: The adverb form is inordinately. «He was inordinately proud of his model boat.» His pride was far bigger than the boat deserved, so inordinately tells you the pride was too much.
fact: Inordinate means much more than is usual, reasonable or expected.
fact: Inordinate is a formal word, so you will see it in reports and exam writing.
fact: «Inordinate» can describe an amount, as in «an inordinate amount of time», or a feeling, as in «inordinately proud».
fact: «Inordinate» means too much: it suggests the amount goes past what is fair or sensible.
fact: The adverb «inordinately» means in a way that is far too much.
exercise: «The teacher said the bus was late for an inordinate length of time.» What does inordinate mean here? | Just right | A small bit | *Far too much | Happy
exercise: Which word means far too much in «He spent an inordinate amount of money on sweets»? | *inordinate | spent | money | sweets
exercise: Jo says inordinately is just a fancy way of saying very. Why is that not quite right? | It means exactly the same as very, so there is no difference | *It means in a way that is far too much, which is more than very | It means quickly | It is the opposite of very
test: «The children waited an inordinate time for the bus.» What does an inordinate time mean? | Just a few minutes | Exactly on time | *Far too long | Not at all
test: Which phrase means far too much? | a small slice | *inordinate delays | perfectly fine | just right
exam: Which sentence uses inordinate to mean far too much? | *«The homework took an inordinate amount of time.» | «The homework was fine and quick.» | «She ate an apple at break.» | «The homework was lost in the bag.»
